<p class="eyebrow">Fortress revival &middot; coordinator skill &middot; skill drafts &middot; version 17 &middot; 6 November 2026</p>
<h1>Your held rules, version 17</h1>
<p class="standfirst">Version 17. One landed change that you have not read, item 27, now in three sentences: before each update, the page worker reads every comment on the page; a plain &ldquo;LGTM&rdquo; on a landed item is your word that you read it; every other comment goes to the coordinator. Nothing waits for your yes.</p>
</header>

<nav class="parts" aria-label="Sections">
<h2>Sections</h2>
<ul>
<li><a href="#s1">Landed since your last approval</a></li>
<li><a href="#s2">Proposed changes</a></li>
<li><a href="#words">Words used on this page</a></li>
</ul>
</nav>

<section id="s1" class="part">
<h2>Landed since your last approval</h2>
<p>You read item 26 at 10:29 UTC on 6 November 2026, so it is not on the page.</p>
<p>One item, 27, a change to the coordinator skill that you have not read. It shows the change against the text before it.</p>
<p>In the item, the words that changed are underlined, and the words that went are struck through. To reverse a change, send a comment to Claude on the item, or say so in chat. The skill writer then restores the old words.</p>

<section id="l27" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 27 &middot; skill-rewrite.md, &ldquo;The method&rdquo;, step 7</span>The page worker reads every comment before an update, in three sentences</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, the words that changed; struck through, the words that went. In the skill's words, &ldquo;you&rdquo; is the coordinator.</span>
<p class="where-in">skill-rewrite.md, &ldquo;The method&rdquo;, step 7, lines 19 to 26</p>
<ol start="7"><li>After each change to the coordinator skill or to <code>fortress-repo</code> lands, have a worker update that skill's page: the page of skill drafts for the coordinator skill, the page of <code>fortress-repo</code> changes for <code>fortress-repo</code>. The boot note holds both links. Give the worker the page's URL, and have it publish by that URL. The record-landing workflow runs this update itself for the changes to <code>fortress-repo</code> that follow a landing (<code>references/batch.md</code>, &ldquo;The landing&rdquo;, step 4). The page of skill drafts has two sections, and the page of <code>fortress-repo</code> changes has only the first:
<ul>
<li>Landed changes: only the landed changes that the curator has not read, so that no change lands unseen while the curator is away. The items stand oldest first, numbered on from the page's last item, so every item that the curator finds there is new:
<ul>
<li>If the curator says, on the page or in chat, that they have read the page up to an item, the next update removes that item and every older item. <span class="add">Before each update, the worker reads every comment thread on the page, sent to Claude or not. A comment on a landed item that only says that the curator read it, such as &ldquo;LGTM&rdquo;, is such a word, at the comment&rsquo;s time;</span> <span class="cut">Write that item and the time of the word</span> <span class="add">write the newest such word and its time</span> in the boot note&rsquo;s &ldquo;Pages&rdquo; line of that page. <span class="add">Every other comment, a yes on a draft among them, drops no item, and the worker quotes it to you.</span> The page worker and the record-landing workflow's page stage both read it there. You may have the update made at once, without waiting for the next landing.</li>
<li>A draft is an item under &ldquo;Proposed changes&rdquo; that waits for the curator's yes. Its landing, the commit of the approved words, gets no item, unless it fixed a hit of the skills' lint, <code>explorations/coordinator/tools/skills-lint.py</code>, or a wrong path or section name: then one item shows the fix alone. The skill writer first runs the lint and checks the draft's paths and section names; the fix needs no second cold read. Any other change is a change of its own and goes the way its kind goes.</li>
<li>Every other item stays until the curator says, on the page or in chat, that they have read it.</li>
</ul>
<p>The item takes the form of a landed item in <code>SKILL.md</code>, &ldquo;Pages&rdquo;. It shows the change against the skill's text before it, or, for an approved draft whose words changed at the landing, against the approved words. It gives the change's reason, which may come from a cold read, a hit of the skills' lint, a fix at a draft's landing, or the record entry that the orientation workflow, in update mode, took in. The reason says in plain words what was wrong and what the text now says. It never cites a cold read's flag by its code or number, a judge's ruling by its number, or an item of a task by its number.</p></li>
<li>Proposed changes: the items that wait for the curator's yes. When an approved item lands, a worker takes it off this section.</li>
</ul></li></ol>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> At 10:29 UTC on 6 November you wrote &ldquo;LGTM.&rdquo; on item 26 without sending it to Claude, and the page kept showing item 26 as unread: no one read the page&rsquo;s comments before an update. The rule now has the worker read every comment thread on the page before each update, take a comment on a landed item that only says you read it as your word, at its time, and quote every other comment to the coordinator. At 11:31 you asked whether the rule needs to be so long; it is cut to these three sentences. How the worker uses the comment tool, its cursor and what to do without it stay in the worker&rsquo;s brief.</p></div>
</section>

<div class="sources"><span class="label">References</span>
<ul>
<li>Item 27: commits of 6 November, <code>e95fd785e</code>, 11:02 UTC, &ldquo;coordinator: the page worker reads every comment before an update&rdquo;; <code>adbbf2051</code>, 11:09 UTC, &ldquo;coordinator: the cold read&rsquo;s fixes on the page-comments rule&rdquo;; <code>760864d79</code>, 11:10 UTC, &ldquo;coordinator: the page worker lists every comment thread by the cursor&rdquo;; and <code>edaa59577</code>, 11:32 UTC, &ldquo;coordinator: the page-comments rule cut to three sentences&rdquo;. Shown as the net change of the four against the text before <code>e95fd785e</code>. Your comments of 10:29 and 10:59 UTC are on item 26&rsquo;s thread on this page, and your question of 11:31 UTC on item 27&rsquo;s.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>

<section id="s2" class="part">
<h2>Proposed changes</h2>
<p>Nothing waits for your yes. You approved draft 14, in its version 4, at 10:42 UTC on 6 November, and its twelve passages landed in commit <code>551b02858</code> with the words that you approved: neither the skills&rsquo; lint nor a check of its paths and section names changed a word, so its landing has no item under &ldquo;Landed since your last approval&rdquo;.</p>
</section>

<section id="words" class="part">
<h2>Words used on this page</h2>
<dl class="gloss">
<dt>coordinator</dt><dd>Coordinating session.</dd>
<dt>read mark</dt><dd>Your word that you read the page up to an item, such as a plain &ldquo;LGTM&rdquo; on a landed item, which drops that item and every older one at the next update.</dd>
<dt>boot note</dt><dd>File of what is in flight now, which the coordinator reads at each boot, with the links of the pages.</dd>
<dt>draft</dt><dd>Item under “Proposed changes” that waits for your yes.</dd>
<dt>page stage</dt><dd>Stage of the record-landing workflow that updates the page of <code>fortress-repo</code> changes.</dd>
<dt>diff</dt><dd>Text that shows what a change cut and added.</dd>
<dt>cold read</dt><dd>Reading of a changed part by a worker given only the skill, which marks each passage that would send it wrong or make it search.</dd>
<dt>authorship map</dt><dd>Map of who wrote what in the team's texts, compiled from the git history, in <code>research/authorship.md</code>.</dd>
<dt>cold reader</dt><dd>Worker that does a cold read.</dd>
<dt>flag</dt><dd>Passage that a cold read marks, with its problem and a proposed fix.</dd>
<dt>lint</dt><dd>Script that searches the skills' text for internal handles, status words and stale wording.</dd>
<dt>register</dt><dd>Way in which the coordinator writes to you, which the bullets at the head of “Talking to the curator” set.</dd>
<dt><code>fortress-repo</code></dt><dd>Skill that holds the rules that bind the workers in the repository.</dd>
<dt>revival part</dt><dd>Part of <code>fortress-repo</code> that tells workers how revived Fortress differs from the original.</dd>
<dt>closing docket</dt><dd>List of 3 November of the open items of the post-mortems and the held list, each with a proposed close.</dd>
<dt>commit stage</dt><dd>Last stage of a batch, which commits the landed tree and pushes it.</dd>
<dt>workflow journal</dt><dd>Log of a Workflow run, with a line for each agent's start and result.</dd>
<dt><code>spend.py</code></dt><dd>Script that counts the tokens written in the transcripts that changed after a given time.</dd>
<dt>quiz</dt><dd>Small set of decisions that a fresh worker answers from new entries, to test that their meaning stayed.</dd>
<dt>pilot</dt><dd>First run on three or four entries, which you read before the rest run.</dd>
<dt>chunk</dt><dd>Group of items that one writer takes in a round: about ten entries or rows in a long writing pass, 20 to 32 entries in a consolidation.</dd>
<dt>Workflow run</dt><dd>One launch of a workflow script by the Workflow tool, with its own ID and its own agents.</dd>
<dt>writer stream</dt><dd>Line of writer sessions in one run, each resumed while its context is small and its prompt cache warm.</dd>
<dt>judge</dt><dd>Agent at the end of a round that rules apply or hold on each point that a chunk's checks raise.</dd>
<dt>long writing pass</dt><dd>Run of a workflow that rewrites many entries or rows of the record, chunk by chunk.</dd>
<dt>record-landing workflow</dt><dd>Workflow that brings the record in line with a batch's landing.</dd>
<dt>revival page</dt><dd>Page that tells where the revival stands, which the record-landing workflow updates after a landing.</dd>
<dt>mining workflow</dt><dd>Workflow that finds where each POSITIONS entry came from.</dd>
<dt>clarity workflow</dt><dd>Workflow that rewrites entries so that each reads on its own.</dd>
<dt>orientation workflow</dt><dd>Workflow that writes and updates the revival part.</dd>
</dl>
</section>
<footer class="colophon">
<p>Version 1, 5 November 2026: an Opus worker made it at the coordinator's brief, from commits <code>23f18f024</code> and <code>f1dff89b4</code>, the cold read of <code>23f18f024</code> and the skill writer's report.</p>
<p>Version 2: added draft 13 under “Proposed changes”.</p>
<p>Version 3: wrote the reasons in plain words.</p>
<p>Version 4: added the landing of draft 13 and its fixes, from commits <code>e6bc2b83c</code> and <code>b9952aba9</code>.</p>
<p>Version 5: added the skill writer's pass over its leftovers and its fixes after a cold read, items 15 to 19.</p>
<p>Version 6: removed items 1 to 19 and their references, which you read at 16:49 UTC on 5 November.</p>
<p>Version 7: added items 20 to 23, from commits <code>9449ed5aa</code> and <code>5dd90f9fe</code>, made from your comments of 16:38 to 16:49 and a cold read.</p>
<p>Version 8: removed items 20 to 23, which you read at 17:20 UTC on 5 November, and added item 24, from commit <code>95dc79442</code>, and draft 14 under “Proposed changes”.</p>
<p>Version 9: replaced draft 14 with its revision of twelve passages, made from your comment of 17:45 UTC and a second cold read.</p>
<p>Version 10: added item 25, from commits <code>b88d26f52</code> and <code>7e11345b3</code>, the change to step 4 of the landing and a cold read&rsquo;s fixes.</p>
<p>Version 11: replaced draft 14's version 2 with its version 3, made from your comment of 05:28 UTC on 6 November and a third cold read.</p>
<p>Version 12: added item 26, from commits <code>d070554b5</code> and <code>cda4fcdff</code>, the Workflow cap per run and how a long writing pass is split and run, with a cold read&rsquo;s fixes.</p>
<p>Version 13: removed items 24 and 25, which you read at 05:12 and 05:15 UTC on 6 November, and replaced draft 14's version 3 with its version 4, made from your comments of 09:52, 09:55 and 09:57 UTC on 6 November and a fourth cold read, with the skill writer's fixes. Item 26 stays.</p>
<p>Version 14: removed draft 14 from &ldquo;Proposed changes&rdquo;, since you approved its version 4 at 10:42 UTC on 6 November and it landed as approved in commit <code>551b02858</code>, with no word changed; nothing waits for your yes. Item 26 stays.</p>
<p>Version 15: removed item 26 and its references, which you read at 10:29 UTC on 6 November; the page is empty, with nothing landed unread and nothing waiting for your yes.</p>
<p>Version 16: added item 27, from commits <code>e95fd785e</code>, <code>adbbf2051</code> and <code>760864d79</code>, the page worker reading every comment before an update, with a cold read&rsquo;s fixes; item 27 is unread, and nothing waits for your yes.</p>
<p>Version 17: item 27 shows the rule cut to three sentences, from commit <code>edaa59577</code>, after your question of 11:31 UTC; item 27 is unread, and nothing waits for your yes.</p>
<p>This page changes no file in the repository.</p>
