<section id="l27" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 27 &middot; skill-rewrite.md, &ldquo;The method&rdquo;, step 7</span>The page worker reads every comment before an update, in three sentences</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, the words that changed; struck through, the words that went. In the skill's words, &ldquo;you&rdquo; is the coordinator.</span>
<p class="where-in">skill-rewrite.md, &ldquo;The method&rdquo;, step 7, lines 19 to 26</p>
<ol start="7"><li>After each change to the coordinator skill or to <code>fortress-repo</code> lands, have a worker update that skill's page: the page of skill drafts for the coordinator skill, the page of <code>fortress-repo</code> changes for <code>fortress-repo</code>. The boot note holds both links. Give the worker the page's URL, and have it publish by that URL. The record-landing workflow runs this update itself for the changes to <code>fortress-repo</code> that follow a landing (<code>references/batch.md</code>, &ldquo;The landing&rdquo;, step 4). The page of skill drafts has two sections, and the page of <code>fortress-repo</code> changes has only the first:
<ul>
<li>Landed changes: only the landed changes that the curator has not read, so that no change lands unseen while the curator is away. The items stand oldest first, numbered on from the page's last item, so every item that the curator finds there is new:
<ul>
<li>If the curator says, on the page or in chat, that they have read the page up to an item, the next update removes that item and every older item. <span class="add">Before each update, the worker reads every comment thread on the page, sent to Claude or not. A comment on a landed item that only says that the curator read it, such as &ldquo;LGTM&rdquo;, is such a word, at the comment&rsquo;s time;</span> <span class="cut">Write that item and the time of the word</span> <span class="add">write the newest such word and its time</span> in the boot note&rsquo;s &ldquo;Pages&rdquo; line of that page. <span class="add">Every other comment, a yes on a draft among them, drops no item, and the worker quotes it to you.</span> The page worker and the record-landing workflow's page stage both read it there. You may have the update made at once, without waiting for the next landing.</li>
<li>A draft is an item under &ldquo;Proposed changes&rdquo; that waits for the curator's yes. Its landing, the commit of the approved words, gets no item, unless it fixed a hit of the skills' lint, <code>explorations/coordinator/tools/skills-lint.py</code>, or a wrong path or section name: then one item shows the fix alone. The skill writer first runs the lint and checks the draft's paths and section names; the fix needs no second cold read. Any other change is a change of its own and goes the way its kind goes.</li>
<li>Every other item stays until the curator says, on the page or in chat, that they have read it.</li>
</ul>
<p>The item takes the form of a landed item in <code>SKILL.md</code>, &ldquo;Pages&rdquo;. It shows the change against the skill's text before it, or, for an approved draft whose words changed at the landing, against the approved words. It gives the change's reason, which may come from a cold read, a hit of the skills' lint, a fix at a draft's landing, or the record entry that the orientation workflow, in update mode, took in. The reason says in plain words what was wrong and what the text now says. It never cites a cold read's flag by its code or number, a judge's ruling by its number, or an item of a task by its number.</p></li>
<li>Proposed changes: the items that wait for the curator's yes. When an approved item lands, a worker takes it off this section.</li>
</ul></li></ol>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> At 10:29 UTC on 6 November you wrote &ldquo;LGTM.&rdquo; on item 26 without sending it to Claude, and the page kept showing item 26 as unread: no one read the page&rsquo;s comments before an update. The rule now has the worker read every comment thread on the page before each update, take a comment on a landed item that only says you read it as your word, at its time, and quote every other comment to the coordinator. At 11:31 you asked whether the rule needs to be so long; it is cut to these three sentences. How the worker uses the comment tool, its cursor and what to do without it stay in the worker&rsquo;s brief.</p></div>
